### 5\. Tools that test APIs and backend services

| Tool         | What it does                              |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| Postman      | Sends API requests and examines responses |
| Insomnia     | Tests REST and GraphQL APIs               |
| HTTPie       | Sends HTTP requests                       |
| curl         | Tests endpoints and HTTP responses        |
| Newman       | Automates Postman collections             |
| Schemathesis | Tests APIs against OpenAPI schemas        |

### 6\. Tools for Android system and storage inspection

| Tool                    | What it investigates                           |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| ADB Shell               | Android operating system and shell             |
| SQLite3                 | Application databases                          |
| run-as                  | Private app files for eligible debuggable apps |
| Android Device Explorer | Application files and directories              |
| logcat                  | Android and application logs                   |
| dumpsys                 | Android system services and application state  |
| dumpsys package         | Installed application configuration            |
| dumpsys activity        | Activity and task information                  |
| dumpsys connectivity    | Network state                                  |

### 7\. Which tools can interact with which layers?

This is the important distinction when building your testing laboratory.

| Testing layer                 | Recommended tools      |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Phone control                 | ADB, scrcpy            |
| Screen automation             | Appium, UI Automator   |
| APK inspection                | JADX, APKTool          |
| Runtime execution             | Frida, Objection, LLDB |
| Java debugging                | JDWP                   |
| Network capture               | Wireshark, TCPdump     |
| HTTP/HTTPS interception       | mitmproxy, ZAP         |
| API testing                   | Postman, curl, HTTPie  |
| Storage inspection            | ADB, SQLite3           |
| Automated security assessment | MobSF                  |
| Native code analysis          | Ghidra                 |

### 8\. My recommended toolkit for your Ubuntu + Android 11 lab

Recommended core toolkit

Install and learn these first:

* ADB — control the virtual phone.

* Appium — automate application interaction.

* JADX — examine application code.

* APKTool — examine application resources.

* Frida — inspect runtime behaviour.

* Objection — explore the runtime environment.

* Wireshark — inspect network traffic.

* TCPdump — capture packets.

* MobSF — automate mobile security analysis.

* Postman — test backend APIs.

One important point: No single tool can interact with every part of an application. For example, Appium interacts with the interface, Frida observes runtime execution, JADX examines code, and Wireshark examines network packets.

### 9\. Let me distinguish the kind of interaction you want

Which type of interaction do you want to master?

All types — complete mobile testing toolkit

Control the app like a human: click, type, scroll and navigate

Interact with the running app's internal code and memory

Observe and test everything the app sends and receives

Interact with Android itself: files, permissions, processes and databases

Automate entire application workflows and test cases

Continue with practical guide
